<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch8-section-mid-latitude-synoptic-scale-analysis-subsection-rossby-number" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Rossby number</title>

    <p>
        Since geostrophic balance neglects air parcel horizontal acceleration,
        it can be useful to determine if air parcel horizontal acceleration for various types of atmospheric motions is "small enough"
        so that geostrophic balance can apply with sufficient accuracy,
        or if it is "too large" to meaningfully apply geostrophic balance.
    </p>

    <p>
        The <term>Rossby number</term> <m>Ro</m>
        expresses the ratio of the magnitude of an object's horizontal acceleration to the horizontal Coriolis acceleration.
        In other words,
        it expresses how strongly Earth's rotation affects an object's motion.
    </p>

    <p>
        <xref ref="table-horizontal-equations-of-motion-scaled" text="type-global" />
        shows that an air parcel's horizontal acceleration scales as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            \frac{U}{T} = \dfrac{U}{\sfrac{L}{U}} = \dfrac{U^2}{L}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        and the horizontal Coriolis acceleration scales as
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            f_0 U
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        The ratio of the scaled horizontal acceleration to the scaled horizontal Coriolis acceleration gives <m>Ro</m>,
        as shown in <xref ref="eqn-rossby-number" text="type-global" /> below.
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-rossby-number" number="yes">
            Ro
            =
            \frac{\sfrac{U^2}{L}}{f_0U}
            =
            \frac{U}{f_0L}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Note that <xref ref="eqn-rossby-number" text="type-global" />
        leads to an alternative definition of <m>Ro</m>
        as the ratio of the timescale of Earth's rotation <m>T_{rot} = \dfrac{1}{f}</m>
        to the timescale of fluid flow <m>T_{flow} = \dfrac{L}{U}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        <md number="yes">
            Ro
            =
            \frac{T_{rot}}{T_{flow}}
            =
            \frac{\sfrac{1}{f}}{\sfrac{L}{U}}
            =
            \frac{U}{fL}
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        For mid-latitude synoptic scale motion,
        we expect <m>\dfrac{U^2}{L} \sim 10^{-4} \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-2}</m>
        and <m>f_0 U \sim 10^{-3} \, \text{m} \, \text{s}^{-2}</m>,
        producing <m>Ro = 10^{-1} = 0.1</m>.
        This is a low value of <m>Ro</m>,
        and so we say "low-<m>Ro</m> flows" for which <m>Ro \ll 1</m>
        approximately are in geostrophic balance.
        In other words,
        geostrophic balance reasonably can be used to approximate low-<m>Ro</m> atmospheric motions
        like mid-latitude synoptic scale motions.
    </p>

    <p>
        As you will learn in a later dynamics course,
        "high-<m>Ro</m>" flows for which <m>Ro \gg 1</m>
        are not close to geostrophic
        because air parcels experience horizontal accelerations much greater than the horizontal Coriolis acceleration,
        and the horizontal Coriolis acceleration consequently can be neglected.
        For "intermediate-<m>Ro</m>" flows for which <m>Ro \approx 1</m>,
        air parcel horizontal accelerations are of approximately equal magnitude as the horizontal Coriolis acceleration,
        and so air parcel horizontal acceleration must be incorporated in <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-balance-x-start" text="type-global" />
        and <xref ref="eqn-geostrophic-balance-y-start" text="type-global" />.
        In this case,
        geostrophic balance is not quite a reasonable approximation and must be modified to improve its applicability.
    </p>

</subsection>